tscience.pdf]]"],[15406,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[15421,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[15637,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[15775,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[15835,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[16132,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[16864,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[16870,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[17119,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[17224,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[17350,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[17356,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[17614,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[18964,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[19108,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[19120,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[19123,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[19126,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[19333,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[19771,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[19774,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[19996,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[19999,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[20002,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[20005,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[20008,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[20011,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[20014,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[20017,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[20020,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[20023,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[20149,"[[flightscience.pdf]]"],[2010,"[[flightscience.pdf]]\n\n[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[13027,"[[flightscience.pdf]]\n\n[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[6002,"[[flightscience.pdf]]\n[[geometrycreationandimport.pdf]]"],[8974,"[[flightscience.pdf]]\n[[gps.epub]]"],[9265,"[[flightscience.pdf]]\n[[gps.epub]]"],[20747,"[[Fluid Mechanics and Material Properties]] connects distributed forces to material response. [[Hydrostatic Pressure]] grows with depth because of fluid weight, and [[Archimedes' Principle]] turns the resulting pressure difference into buoyancy. [[Dynamic and Kinematic Viscosity Units|Viscosity]] resists shear and helps determine whether flow remains [[Laminar Flow|laminar]] or becomes [[Turbulent Flow|turbulent]]. The [[Continuity Equation for Fluid Flow]] preserves mass through a changing cross-section, while the [[Bernoulli Equation]] balances pressure, kinetic, and gravitational energy density along an ideal streamline.\n\nSolids respond to load through deformation. [[Hooke's Law]] describes the initial linear restoring relation, [[Young's Modulus]] measures stiffness through stress divided by strain, and a [[Stress-Strain Curve]] distinguishes proportional, elastic, yielding, ultimate-strength, and fracture behavior. These material quantities remove specimen dimensions so different samples of the same substance can be compared.\n\n[[Thermal and Statistical Physics]] links macroscopic state variables to microscopic motion. [[Thermal Equilibrium]] permits temperature comparison. [[Specific Heat Capacity]] measures energy needed for temperature change, whereas [[Specific Latent Heat]] describes energy transferred during a phase change without temperature change. The [[Ideal Gas Equation]] combines pressure, volume, amount, and absolute temperature, while the [[Kinetic Theory of Gases]] explains pressure through molecular impacts and [[Temperature as Molecular Kinetic Energy]].\n\nThe [[First Law of Thermodynamics]] treats heat and work as energy transfers that alter internal energy. The [[Second Law of Thermodynamics]] restricts which energy-conserving changes occur spontaneously. [[Entropy]] describes that direction both through reversible heat transfer and through the multiplicity of microscopic arrangements. A [[Heat Engine]] illustrates the consequence: some absorbed heat can become work, but some must be rejected to a colder reservoir."],[20871,"[[Formal Logic]] studies propositions, operators, equivalence, premises, conclusions, and valid deduction. Boolean algebra turns logical relationships into operations that can be manipulated symbolically and implemented electronically. The value of a formal system is not merely that it produces an answer, but that its syntax and inference rules make the path from assumptions to conclusion inspectable.\n\nFormalization also reveals limits. A valid deduction can still begin from false premises, and a precisely defined model can omit relevant features of the world. Mathematics separates internal correctness from empirical adequacy: proof establishes what follows inside a system, while observation determines whether that system is a useful representation of its subject."],[20873,"[[Foundations of Mathematics]] connects elementary real-number algebra with analytic and Euclidean geometry, polynomial equations, functions, trigonometry, limits, derivatives, and spherical geometry. Its progression shows how arithmetic laws justify symbolic transformations, coordinates translate figures into equations, functions express dependence, limits control approximation, and derivatives describe local change. The final spatial topics use vector products and great-circle geometry to contrast plane results with triangles drawn on a sphere."],[855,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[996,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[1002,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[1062,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[1212,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[1215,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[1218,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[1239,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[1398,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[2064,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[2370,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[2439,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[2448,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[3695,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[4553,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[4598,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[4715,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[4718,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[4724,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[4730,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[5261,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[5288,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[5291,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[5408,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[5411,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[5540,"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2